 ## Background
-The standard volume rendering equation, often referred to as the "Max formula" in the context of neural rendering comparisons, is derived from the integral form of the radiative transfer equation [S2, S3]. It was popularized in computer graphics for rendering participating media such as fog, smoke, and clouds [S3]. The specific discrete integration routine described here, which treats opacity as ray-dependent, is the standard approach in many volumetric rendering implementations [S2, S3]. It is distinct from the "Neural Volumes" formula, which was proposed for neural implicit representations but is less physically accurate in terms of opacity accumulation [S2].
